    "toolsDetail.internet.p": "The only way out to the web. Every request goes through a proxy that logs it, so a page that tries to manipulate an agent is caught where it comes in.",
    "toolsDetail.bus.p": "How agents talk to each other and to the orchestrator. Text only, so a compromised agent can't pass code or orders to the rest without it being seen.",
    "toolsDetail.runtime.p": "Where agents run code. Each task gets a fresh sandbox with no network, so whatever runs there can't get out or outlive the task.",
    "toolsDetail.fs.p": "The task's own folders. Agents read and write there but can't delete or open secrets, so a bad run leaves traces instead of damage.",
    "layersTable.tradeoff": "Trade-offs",
    "ui.stp": "How do we shrink the attack surface as much as possible with dead-simple tool implementations? Do we inject controls on the fly that spot adversarial attempts by the swarm to get around the restrictions? How do we stay scalable for swarms of 10,000 or 100,000 agents working in parallel?",
    "controlsDetail.tool.p": "Each tool has its own limits and alarms. A call that breaks a rule is blocked and the CONTEXT is flagged or isolated from the bus and tools.",
    "controlsDetail.toolLayer.p": "A layer every tool call goes through. It enforces budgets, pauses before any irreversible action, checks what goes out and, when in doubt, blocks.",
    "controlsDetail.access.p": "Defines what each agent can reach: allowed sites, usage limits and its own credentials. Nothing is shared between agents.",
